# OpenCandy (2008-2016). An advertising library inside free-software installers that offered extra programs.
# A quest (QUESTS.md): the trainee converts call recordings at work, installs the approved tool safely, then meets OpenCandy
# inside the installer of a free converter. The victim is invented: OpenCandy hit anyone who installed free software.
os windows
user Dana Okafor
about
  | OpenCandy was a small advertising library that free-software makers built into their installers. In the middle of the setup it added a "Recommended software" page.
  | Accepting it downloaded and installed another company's program. OpenCandy's helper told the advertiser about each install, and the advertiser paid for it.
  | The training copy shows the helper as its own program, so you can find it in {Task Manager|Activity Monitor}. In the real setup it ran inside the installer. Dana's job is invented: the real victims were anyone who installed a free program.
story
  | You are Dana Okafor, office coordinator at Northwind Traders. This week you turn five recorded customer calls into MP3 files and upload them to the training library.
  | It is Thursday afternoon. You have finished 3 of the 5 recordings. Two to go.
  | Your portal sign-in and the call list are in two text files on the desktop. That is a bad habit, but it keeps this training quick.
quota 3 5 Recordings finished

