step What the offer left behind
  look the animation, and {Task Manager|Activity Monitor}: the helper's network activity, and the notice
  | Dana wanted one converter. She also got a program that starts with {Windows|your Mac}, nags for $29.95, and tried to report each install to an advertiser. A toolbar and a home page now show ads.
  | The training desktop has no network, so the report was blocked. On a real computer it would have gone out.
open taskmanager
block offers.opencandy.invalid 80
attack spyware-surveillance
  | Free Audio Converter installed
  | Two boxes ticked for you
  | PC Speed Optimizer, started with the PC
  | home page changed to a search site
  | install reported to an advertiser

flag hard section download 1
  | The details say partner offers are shown during setup. That is the advertiser.
flag hard line ac-setup ticked above
  label Offers ticked before you saw them
  | Both boxes were ticked for you. Clicking Next installs them, so unticking them was the only way to say no.
flag hard process OCSetupHlp.exe
  | OpenCandy's helper, started by the installer. It tries to report the offer to the advertiser.
flag hard process PCSpeedOptimizer.exe
  | The extra program the Recommended page installed. Dana came for a converter, not for this.
flag hard startup PCSpeedOptimizer
  | The extra program added itself to the programs that start with {Windows|your Mac}.
flag hard section speedsearch 1
  label Sponsored results on a search page Dana never chose
  | The search page became Dana's home page without her choosing it. Its adverts were paid for by the offer.
flag hard section library 2
  | Adverts inside the company's own page. The toolbar added by PC Speed Optimizer put them there.
flag soft file Desktop/portal-login.txt
  | A portal password in a text file on the desktop: any program that runs can read it. Use a password manager.
flag soft file Desktop/call-list.txt
  | Customer account numbers in a loose text file. They belong in the company system only.

lesson
  | In an installer, "Recommended" means the advertiser paid for it. Clear every ticked box you did not come for.
  | Choose "Custom" or "Advanced" install when it is offered: that is where the extras are. A plain installer, like IT's, offers nothing else.
  | "PC speed" and "cleaner" programs offered this way are almost never worth having. Remove them in {Settings > Apps|the Applications folder}.
